##:
net/tcp -- TcpStream and TcpListener over one file descriptor.

The module ships as bundled .sushi source and is merged as a compilation unit
when imported (`use <net/tcp>`). It composes the <net/socket> primitives; a
struct here is a typed name for a descriptor and carries nothing else.

There is NO RAII for a socket, exactly as there is none for a file. A
TcpStream owns no heap, so it COPIES, and a copy holds the same descriptor.
The rule is that ONE binding owns a socket and tcp_close(poke s) is what ends
it. A close writes -1 into the binding it was given, so closing that binding
again is a success; a copy taken before the close keeps a number the kernel
has freed and may reuse, and an operation on it is a bug this compiler cannot
catch.

Limitations: blocking sockets only, so tcp_connect has no timeout of its own
and an unreachable address waits for the kernel to give up. tcp_recv answers
whatever one read gave, which may be less than the maximum asked for;
tcp_recv_exact is the loop for a caller that needs a count.
:##

use <net/socket>

##:
One connected TCP stream.
:##
public struct TcpStream:
    ##: The descriptor, or -1 once tcp_close has run. :##
    i32 fd

##:
One listening TCP socket.
:##
public struct TcpListener:
    ##: The descriptor, or -1 once tcp_listener_close has run. :##
    i32 fd

##:
Connects to a host and port.

The host may be a name or a numeric address; a name is resolved and every
answer is tried in turn. There is no connect timeout, so an address that
answers nothing waits for the kernel.

- Parameter host: The host to reach, as a name or an address.
- Parameter port: The port to connect to.
- Returns: The connected stream.
- Errors: NetError, when no answer for the host accepted a connection.
:##
public fn tcp_connect(string host, i32 port) TcpStream | NetError:
    let i32 fd = sock_tcp_connect(host, port)??
    return Result.Ok(TcpStream(fd))

##:
Binds a listening socket and starts accepting connections.

An empty host means the wildcard address. Port 0 asks the kernel to choose,
and tcp_local_port reads back what it chose. SO_REUSEADDR is always set, so a
port whose last connection is still closing binds again at once.

- Parameter host: The address to bind, or an empty string for the wildcard.
- Parameter port: The port to bind, or 0 to let the kernel choose.
- Parameter backlog: How many completed connections may wait to be accepted.
- Returns: The listening socket.
- Errors: NetError, when the address cannot be bound.
:##
public fn tcp_listen(string host, i32 port, i32 backlog) TcpListener | NetError:
    let i32 fd = sock_tcp_listen(host, port, backlog)??
    return Result.Ok(TcpListener(fd))

##:
Takes the next connection waiting on a listener.

The call blocks until one arrives, unless tcp_listener_set_timeout has given
the listener a bound, in which case it answers TimedOut.

- Parameter l: The listener to take a connection from.
- Returns: The accepted stream.
- Errors: NetError, when no connection could be taken.
:##
public fn tcp_accept(TcpListener l) TcpStream | NetError:
    let i32 fd = sock_tcp_accept(l.fd)??
    return Result.Ok(TcpStream(fd))

##:
Writes bytes, and answers how many of them went.

One write may take fewer bytes than it was offered. A caller that needs the
whole buffer gone wants tcp_send_all instead.

- Parameter s: The stream to write to.
- Parameter data: The bytes to write. The buffer stays the caller's.
- Returns: How many bytes were written.
- Errors: NetError, when the write failed.
:##
public fn tcp_send(TcpStream s, u8[] data) i32 | NetError:
    let i32 sent = sock_send(s.fd, data)??
    return Result.Ok(sent)

##:
Writes every byte, looping until the buffer is gone.

- Parameter s: The stream to write to.
- Parameter data: The bytes to write. The buffer stays the caller's.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when a write failed before the buffer was gone.
:##
public fn tcp_send_all(TcpStream s, u8[] data) ~ | NetError:
    let i32 total = data.len()
    let i32 done = 0
    while (done < total):
        let u8[] rest = data.ss(done, total - done)
        let i32 sent = sock_send(s.fd, rest)??
        if (sent <= 0):
            return Result.Err(NetError.Closed)
        done := done + sent
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Reads what one read gives, up to a maximum.

An empty answer means the peer closed cleanly. A timeout is an error rather
than an empty answer, so the two never read alike.

- Parameter s: The stream to read from.
- Parameter max: The most bytes to take in this one read.
- Returns: The bytes that arrived, empty when the peer closed.
- Errors: NetError, when the read failed.
:##
public fn tcp_recv(TcpStream s, i32 max) u8[] | NetError:
    let u8[] got = sock_recv(s.fd, max)??
    return Result.Ok(got)

##:
Reads exactly `count` bytes, looping until they have all arrived.

A peer that closes early is an error, not a short answer: a caller asking for
an exact count has no use for a partial one.

- Parameter s: The stream to read from.
- Parameter count: How many bytes to wait for.
- Returns: Exactly `count` bytes.
- Errors: NetError.Closed when the peer closed early, or the read's own error.
:##
public fn tcp_recv_exact(TcpStream s, i32 count) u8[] | NetError:
    let u8[] out = from([])
    while (out.len() < count):
        let u8[] chunk = sock_recv(s.fd, count - out.len())??
        if (chunk.len() == 0):
            return Result.Err(NetError.Closed)
        out.extend(chunk)
    return Result.Ok(out)

##:
The address of the peer at the other end, as text.

The text is numeric and no resolver is asked, so this makes no network
request.

- Parameter s: The connected stream.
- Returns: The peer's address.
- Errors: NetError, when the stream has no peer.
:##
public fn tcp_peer_ip(TcpStream s) string | NetError:
    let string ip = sock_peer_ip(s.fd)??
    return Result.Ok(ip)

##:
The port of the peer at the other end.

- Parameter s: The connected stream.
- Returns: The peer's port.
- Errors: NetError, when the stream has no peer.
:##
public fn tcp_peer_port(TcpStream s) i32 | NetError:
    let i32 port = sock_peer_port(s.fd)??
    return Result.Ok(port)

##:
The port a listener actually bound.

This is the only way to learn the port after binding port 0.

- Parameter l: The listening socket.
- Returns: The bound port.
- Errors: NetError, when the socket is not bound.
:##
public fn tcp_local_port(TcpListener l) i32 | NetError:
    let i32 port = sock_local_port(l.fd)??
    return Result.Ok(port)

##:
The port a connected stream bound at this end.

- Parameter s: The connected stream.
- Returns: The local port.
- Errors: NetError, when the socket is not bound.
:##
public fn tcp_stream_local_port(TcpStream s) i32 | NetError:
    let i32 port = sock_local_port(s.fd)??
    return Result.Ok(port)

##:
Bounds how long a read and a write on a stream may wait.

A bound that expires answers NetError.TimedOut. Without one a read waits for
as long as the peer stays silent.

- Parameter s: The stream to bound.
- Parameter recv_ms: The read bound, in milliseconds.
- Parameter send_ms: The write bound, in milliseconds.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when the option could not be set.
:##
public fn tcp_set_timeouts(TcpStream s, i32 recv_ms, i32 send_ms) ~ | NetError:
    sock_set_recv_timeout(s.fd, recv_ms)??
    sock_set_send_timeout(s.fd, send_ms)??
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Bounds how long tcp_accept may wait for a connection.

- Parameter l: The listener to bound.
- Parameter accept_ms: The bound, in milliseconds.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when the option could not be set.
:##
public fn tcp_listener_set_timeout(TcpListener l, i32 accept_ms) ~ | NetError:
    sock_set_recv_timeout(l.fd, accept_ms)??
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Closes a stream and writes -1 into the binding.

The binding is marked so that closing it again is a success. A COPY of the
stream taken before this call still holds the old descriptor, which the kernel
may have given to somebody else; one binding owns a socket.

- Parameter s: The stream to close. It reads -1 afterwards.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when the descriptor could not be closed.
:##
public fn tcp_close(poke TcpStream s) ~ | NetError:
    if (s.fd < 0):
        return Result.Ok(~)
    let i32 fd = s.fd
    s.fd := -1
    sock_close(fd)??
    return Result.Ok(~)

##:
Closes a listener and writes -1 into the binding.

- Parameter l: The listener to close. It reads -1 afterwards.
- Returns: Nothing.
- Errors: NetError, when the descriptor could not be closed.
:##
public fn tcp_listener_close(poke TcpListener l) ~ | NetError:
    if (l.fd < 0):
        return Result.Ok(~)
    let i32 fd = l.fd
    l.fd := -1
    sock_close(fd)??
    return Result.Ok(~)
